TASK 9 – Introduce Abstraction (4 Marks)
1. Abstract Class – Vehicle
An abstract class is used when common properties and behaviours are shared by multiple subclasses.
Vehicle is an abstract class because every vehicle has common details, but its parking rate differs.
•	Fields: regNo, vehicleType
•	Abstract method: calculateFee()
•	Normal method: getRegNo()
2. Interface – Chargeable
An interface defines a capability that a class can implement.
Chargeable is used for electric vehicles because they require an additional charging fee.
•	Method: chargingFee()
•	ElectricCar implements the Chargeable interface.
3. Java Program
abstract class Vehicle {
    String regNo;
    Vehicle(String regNo) {
        this.regNo = regNo;
    }
    abstract double calculateFee();
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle: " + regNo);
    }
}
interface Chargeable {
    double chargingFee(int kWh);
    default void chargingStatus() {
        System.out.println("Charging is available");
    }

    static void displayMessage() {
        System.out.println("EV Charging Facility");
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {

    Car(String regNo) {
        super(regNo);
    }

    @Override
    double calculateFee() {
        return 100;
    }
}

class ElectricCar extends Car implements Chargeable {

    ElectricCar(String regNo) {
        super(regNo);
    }
    @Override
    public double chargingFee(int kWh) {
        return kWh * 12;
    }
}
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ElectricCar e = new ElectricCar("TN01AB1234");
        e.display();
        System.out.println("Parking Fee: " + e.calculateFee());
        System.out.println("Charging Fee: " + e.chargingFee(10));
        e.chargingStatus();
        Chargeable.displayMessage();
    }
}
Output:
Vehicle: TN01AB1234
Parking Fee: 100.0
Charging Fee: 120.0
Charging is available
EV Charging Facility
4. Abstract Class vs Interface
Abstract Class	Interface
Used for common properties and behaviours.	Used for common capabilities.
Can have constructors.	Cannot have constructors.
Can have instance variables.	Fields are public, static and final.
Supports inheritance using extends.	Supports multiple interfaces using implements.
A class can extend only one class.	A class can implement multiple interfaces.
Why both are used?
•	Abstract class Vehicle stores common vehicle details.
•	Interface Chargeable provides charging capability.
•	A class can implement multiple interfaces to obtain multiple behaviours.
5. Default and Static Methods in Interfaces
•	Default method: Provides a common implementation that implementing classes can use or override.
•	Static method: Provides a utility method that can be called using the interface name.
Example: chargingStatus() and displayMessage().
6. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
If Vehicle becomes an interface and Chargeable becomes an abstract class:
•	Vehicle interface can provide abstract and default methods.
•	Chargeable abstract class can store charging details and have constructors.
•	ElectricCar can implement Vehicle and extend Chargeable.
However, Java allows a class to extend only one abstract class. Therefore, it cannot extend another class at the same time.
Comparison	Original Design	Alternative Design
Vehicle	Abstract class	Interface
Chargeable	Interface	Abstract class
Constructors	Vehicle supports constructors	Vehicle interface cannot have constructors
Multiple inheritance	Multiple interfaces supported	Only one abstract class can be extended
Method time complexity	O(1)	O(1)
Object space	Stores instance fields	Stores instance fields inherited from classes

